---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const matrixCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'a', label: 'matrix a (row 1, col 1)', min: -2, max: 2, step: 0.1, value: 0.7 },
    { name: 'b', label: 'matrix b (row 1, col 2)', min: -2, max: 2, step: 0.1, value: -0.7 },
    { name: 'c', label: 'matrix c (row 2, col 1)', min: -2, max: 2, step: 0.1, value: 0.7 },
    { name: 'd', label: 'matrix d (row 2, col 2)', min: -2, max: 2, step: 0.1, value: 0.7 },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'image of point (1, 0): x', expr: 'a' },
    { label: 'image of point (1, 0): y', expr: 'c' },
    { label: 'determinant (area scale)', expr: 'a*d - b*c' },
    { label: 'area preserved when |det| = 1', expr: 'abs(a*d - b*c)' },
  ],
};

const laLinearSort = {
  "prompt": "Which of these transformations are linear? Linear means lines stay lines and the origin stays fixed.",
  "buckets": [
    "linear",
    "not linear"
  ],
  "items": [
    {
      "label": "rotation by 30° about the origin",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "reflection in the x-axis",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "shear that slides the top of the plane sideways",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "translation by (1, 2) — every point shifted",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "squaring both coordinates: (x, y) → (x², y²)",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "projection onto the x-axis",
      "bucket": 0
    }
  ],
  "why": "Linear maps fix the origin and preserve combinations; a translation moves the origin, and squaring bends lines into curves."
};

const laMatrixMatch = {
  "prompt": "Pair each transformation with its matrix:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "rotation by 90° anticlockwise",
      "right": "[[0, −1], [1, 0]]"
    },
    {
      "left": "reflection in the x-axis",
      "right": "[[1, 0], [0, −1]]"
    },
    {
      "left": "enlargement by factor 3",
      "right": "[[3, 0], [0, 3]]"
    },
    {
      "left": "horizontal shear",
      "right": "[[1, 1], [0, 1]]"
    }
  ],
  "why": "Columns are the images of (1,0) and (0,1); once both are known, the whole map follows."
};

const laRopeEst = {
  "prompt": "A 100 N weight hangs from two ropes at 60° on either side of vertical. What tension does each rope carry?",
  "unit": "N",
  "answer": 100,
  "factor": 1.1,
  "why": "Equilibrium: 2T cos 60° = 100, so T = 100 N. At 45° it is 70.7 N; at 80°, about 288 N — shallow ropes are dangerous.",
  "context": "each rope's useful vertical fraction is cos 60° = 0.5"
};

const laDotCloze = {
  "text": "For a = (3, 4) and b = (4, −3), the dot product is %%0%%, so cos θ = %%1%% and the vectors are %%2%%.",
  "answers": [
    [
      "0"
    ],
    [
      "0"
    ],
    [
      "perpendicular",
      "orthogonal",
      "at right angles"
    ]
  ],
  "hint": "Multiply matching components and add: 3×4 + 4×(−3)."
};

const laProjectionOrder = {
  "prompt": "Rebuild least-squares regression as a projection:",
  "items": [
    "Stack the observed responses into a vector b",
    "The predictor columns span a subspace of observation space",
    "Project b onto that subspace — the fitted values p",
    "The residual b − p is orthogonal to every predictor column",
    "Solve the normal equations AᵀA x = Aᵀb for the coefficients"
  ],
  "why": "Orthogonality of the residual is the defining property of a projection, and it is exactly what the normal equations encode."
};

const laDiagSpot = {
  "prompt": "Find the line where the parallelogram proof goes wrong:",
  "lines": [
    "Let A, B, C, D have position vectors a, b, c, d.",
    "The midpoint of AC is (a + c)/2; the midpoint of BD is (b + d)/2.",
    "ABCD is a parallelogram, so AB = DC: b − a = c − d.",
    "Rearranging gives a + c = b + d, so the two diagonals share a midpoint.",
    "Therefore the diagonals bisect each other — but only because ABCD is a rectangle."
  ],
  "errorLine": 4,
  "correction": "The rectangle is irrelevant: the proof used only AB = DC, which holds for every parallelogram.",
  "why": "No right angle was ever used; the argument is valid for any parallelogram, rectangle or not."
};

const laSolveBranch = {
  "root": "You must solve a sparse system of 10,000 linear equations in 10,000 unknowns. Choose a route.",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "text": "The coefficient matrix is sparse and invertible. What do you reach for?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Gaussian elimination with a sparse ordering",
          "next": "elim",
          "feedback": "Yes — elimination exploits sparsity and scales; this is what structural and weather solvers do."
        },
        {
          "label": "Compute the inverse matrix, then multiply",
          "next": "inverse",
          "feedback": "Inverting is far more expensive and fills in zeros; elimination solves directly."
        },
        {
          "label": "Cramer's rule with determinants",
          "next": "cramer",
          "feedback": "Cramer's rule is exponential in the number of unknowns — fine for 2×2, hopeless here."
        }
      ]
    },
    "elim": {
      "text": "Direct elimination (with a good ordering) is the workhorse; iterative methods take over when the system is enormous."
    },
    "inverse": {
      "text": "Forming A⁻¹ costs more than solving Ax = b once, and it destroys sparsity.",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "So what should be done instead?",
          "next": "elim",
          "feedback": "Factorise or eliminate directly — never invert explicitly."
        }
      ]
    },
    "cramer": {
      "text": "Cramer's rule needs one determinant per unknown: 10,000 determinants of size 10,000.",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "What is the practical alternative?",
          "next": "elim",
          "feedback": "Elimination, exploiting the zeros."
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  "why": "Solution method follows structure: sparsity favours elimination; explicit inverses are almost never the right move."
};

const laSvdCompare = {
  "prompt": "Two decompositions — drag the divider:",
  "before": {
    "label": "eigendecomposition",
    "text": "A = QΛQ⁻¹ needs a square matrix and a full set of eigenvectors; defective matrices refuse."
  },
  "after": {
    "label": "SVD",
    "text": "A = UΣVᵀ works for every matrix, square or rectangular; singular values rank the information."
  },
  "why": "SVD trades eigenvectors for two orthonormal bases, which is why it always exists — and why truncating small singular values is the provably best low-rank approximation."
};

const laEigenJudge = {
  "statements": [
    {
      "s": "An eigenvector is a direction the matrix stretches or shrinks but never rotates.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "Av = λv: the output lies on the same line as the input."
    },
    {
      "s": "PageRank's ranking vector is the eigenvector of the link matrix with eigenvalue 1.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "A steady state of the random surfer is exactly a fixed direction of the link matrix."
    },
    {
      "s": "A negative real part of an eigenvalue means disturbances grow without bound.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "Negative real part means decay toward equilibrium; growth needs a positive real part."
    }
  ]
};

const laSpanMatch = {
  "prompt": "Pair each object with why it counts as a vector space:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "functions",
      "right": "add pointwise and scale; Fourier series writes a signal in a basis of sines"
    },
    {
      "left": "polynomials",
      "right": "finite-dimensional, with basis 1, x, x², …"
    },
    {
      "left": "quantum states",
      "right": "a Hilbert space; measurement is projection onto a basis direction"
    },
    {
      "left": "images",
      "right": "a million-dimensional vector; PCA finds the important directions"
    }
  ],
  "why": "The axioms never mention arrows or coordinates — anything that adds and scales obeying the rules is a vector space, and the geometry of span, basis and projection carries over."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/expert/linear-algebra">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This expert lesson collects several threads into one machine. From the advanced tier,
    <em>Vectors and matrices</em> introduced vectors as arrows and matrices as grids — here
    those two pictures fuse into a single idea, the transformation. From the intermediate tier,
    <em>Trigonometry</em> supplied the sine and cosine that the rotation matrix needs, and
    <em>Circles and coordinate geometry</em> gave the geometric facts that the later sections
    re-prove algebraically. What is new at this tier is the claim that the algebra is not
    notation for the geometry — it <em>is</em> the geometry, computable.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>read a matrix as a machine — columns as images of the basis vectors — and predict where any vector goes;</li>
    <li>use the determinant as the signed area scale, and explain why zero determinant means no inverse;</li>
    <li>compute tensions, work and angles with components and the dot product, including projection;</li>
    <li>re-prove classical geometry (parallelogram diagonals, trapeziums) with position vectors;</li>
    <li>connect span, basis and rank–nullity to solving systems at any scale.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Linear algebra is the complete theory of <strong>one simple kind of transformation</strong>:
    the kind that maps lines to lines, keeps the origin fixed and turns grid into grid without
    curving it. That seems restrictive, yet it is the most-used mathematics in modern applied
    work — every 3D game frame, every machine-learning forward pass, every quantum computation
    is chains of linear maps, because linear maps are the ones we can always solve exactly.
    The school syllabus's transformations, vectors, magnitudes and vector geometry are the
    on-ramp to that machinery, and the expert view simply refuses to split them: a
    transformation <em>is</em> a matrix, a vector <em>is</em> both arrow and column of numbers,
    and geometry becomes algebra you can compute with.
  </p>
  <Widget no="1" caption="linear or not?" widget="sort-buckets" config={laLinearSort} />

  <Widget no="2" caption="a 2×2 matrix is four knobs on the whole plane — watch the determinant track the area change" widget="sliderLab" config={matrixCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four sliders are the matrix's four entries, and the
    readouts tell the story. The first two readouts show where (1, 0) lands — the matrix's
    first column — and moving slider <em>c</em> while watching it is the fastest way to feel
    that columns are images of basis vectors. The determinant readout is the signed area
    scale: at the defaults it reads about 0.98, close to area-preserving; drive any entry
    toward the value that zeroes it and watch the whole plane collapse onto a line — that is
    a singular matrix, seen rather than memorised.
  </p>

  <p>
    The matrix [[a, b], [c, d]] sends (1, 0) to column one and (0, 1) to column two — which
    fixes where <em>everything</em> goes, since every vector is a combination of those two.
    The determinant is the signed area scale: 1 means area-preserving (a rotation!), 0 means
    collapse, negative means the plane got flipped.
  </p>

  <h2>Transformations: matrices as machines</h2>
  <p>
    Every school transformation (E7.1) — rotation, reflection, enlargement, shear — has a
    matrix, and reading matrices as machines beats memorising them. A rotation by angle θ is
    the matrix [[cos θ, −sin θ], [sin θ, cos θ]]: check it on the basis vectors — (1, 0) goes
    to (cos θ, sin θ), the unit vector at angle θ, and (0, 1) follows to (−sin θ, cos θ),
    which is exactly 90° further round. Composition of transformations is matrix
    multiplication, and the order matters: rotate 90° then reflect in the x-axis lands in a
    different place from reflect-then-rotate, which is why matrix multiplication is not
    commutative. Enlargement by factor 3 is 3 times the identity; a shear [[1, 1], [0, 1]]
    slides the top of the plane sideways. The determinant tells you which is which at a
    glance: rotations have determinant 1 (area preserved), reflections −1 (area preserved but
    orientation flipped), enlargements k² (area scaled by the square, exactly as the geometry
    topic promises), and a zero determinant means the machine flattens the whole plane onto a
    line — information destroyed, which is precisely when equations stop having unique
    solutions.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="pair each transformation with its matrix" widget="match" config={laMatrixMatch} />

  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "every matrix has an inverse, since every transformation
    can be undone." A transformation is undoable exactly when it neither collapses space nor
    flips information — i.e. when its determinant is non-zero. A projection onto the x-axis
    (matrix [[1, 0], [0, 0]], determinant 0) sends the whole plane onto a line, and no
    inverse can resurrect which height each point came from. This is the geometric meaning of
    "singular matrix", and the reason solving Ax = b can fail: some b are simply not in the
    image of a collapsing map.
  </p>
  <p>
    The game-and-graphics industry runs on exactly this algebra at 60 frames per second.
    A 3D scene is chains of matrices: the <em>model</em> matrix places an object in the
    world (translate, rotate, scale — composed into one 4×4 matrix), the <em>view</em>
    matrix is the camera's inverse transform, and the <em>projection</em> matrix
    flattens the scene onto the screen. A modern GPU evaluates millions of these
    products per frame, which is why matrix multiplication is the operation computer
    hardware is optimised around — and why the 2×2 rules learned here (order matters,
    determinant as the area scale, identity does nothing) are, unchanged in content,
    the production mathematics of every game engine and CGI pipeline.
  </p>
  <p><strong>So far:</strong> every school transformation turned out to have a matrix, the determinant classifies them at a glance, and zero determinant is exactly where invertibility and unique solutions die. The next section moves from transforming whole planes to individual arrows and their component bookkeeping.</p>

  <h2>Vectors in two dimensions: arrows with bookkeeping</h2>
  <p>
    A vector (E7.2) is a quantity with magnitude and direction — displacement, velocity,
    force — and components are the technology that turns arrows into arithmetic: add
    vectors by adding components, and the parallelogram law becomes ordinary column addition.
    The classic worked problem shows why this matters: a 100 N weight hangs from two ropes at
    45° on either side of vertical. Equilibrium demands the upward components cancel the
    weight: 2T cos 45° = 100, so each rope carries T = 100 ÷ (2 × 0.7071) ≈ 70.7 N — more
    tension than a single vertical rope would need (100 N)! The shallower the ropes, the worse
    the tension: at 80° from vertical each rope must pull with about 288 N. Anyone rigging a
    stage, a zip line or a washing line is doing this computation, and the riggers' rule
    "avoid shallow angles" is vector algebra with consequences. Vector addition also
    builds the bridge to physics: velocities of boats crossing rivers, aircraft in crosswinds
    and relative motion are all component-wise bookkeeping with a picture attached.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="call the rope tension" widget="estimate" config={laRopeEst} />

  <h2>Magnitude: length, distance, and the dot product</h2>
  <p>
    Magnitude (E7.3) is Pythagoras packaged: for v = (x, y) the length is √(x² + y²), so the
    vector (3, 4) has magnitude 5 — the oldest triple in the book riding inside the modern
    notation. But the deep tool hides next door: the <strong>dot product</strong>, a·b =
    x₁x₂ + y₁y₂ = |a||b|cos θ. One formula computes both lengths (a·a = |a|²) and the angle
    between vectors (cos θ = a·b ÷ (|a||b|)), and its sign is a classifier: positive means an
    acute angle, zero means perpendicular, negative means obtuse. Perpendicularity by
    arithmetic — no protractor, no diagram — is what makes "is this line perpendicular to
    that one?" a two-second question. Physics pays for the formula directly: work done is
    force dotted with displacement, so pulling a sledge 20 m with a 50 N force angled 60° up
    from the ground delivers 50 × 20 × cos 60° = 500 J — the vertical part of your pull does
    nothing useful, and the dot product is exactly the "useful component" machine. Distance
    between points, closest approach, collision avoidance in games: all dot products in a
    loop.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="fill the blanks — perpendicular by arithmetic" widget="cloze" config={laDotCloze} />
  <p>
    Projection is the dot product's geometric act, and it is worth seeing once exactly.
    The component of vector a in the direction of unit vector u is (a·u)u — the shadow a
    casts on u's line. Fitting a straight line to data by least squares is the same
    operation in higher dimensions: the best-fit coefficients solve "project the
    observation vector onto the space spanned by the predictor columns", and the
    residual is whatever the projection misses. Linear regression — the most-run
    computation in statistics — is therefore a projection theorem, and its famous
    normal equations are the geometry written in coordinates. When a data scientist
    says a model "explains 80% of the variance", they are quoting the projection's
    share of the squared length.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="rebuild least squares as a projection" widget="order" config={laProjectionOrder} />
  <p><strong>So far:</strong> components turned arrows into arithmetic, and the dot product turned lengths and angles into two-second computations — with projection as its geometric act and least-squares regression as its highest-paying application. The final section steps back from individual vectors to the structure they generate: span, basis, and the theorem that bookkeeps what a map keeps and destroys.</p>

  <h2>Vector geometry: structure, bases and proof</h2>
  <p>
    Vector geometry (E7.4) — position vectors, ratios, collinearity, proving midpoints and
    parallel lines — is the first taste of the expert viewpoint: replace coordinates with
    relationships. The midpoint of AB is (a + b)/2, and from that one line the classical
    theorems fall out; the diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other because both
    diagonals' midpoints compute to (a + b)/2 — a two-line proof replacing a page of
    Euclidean argument. Beyond the school version waits the structure that makes linear
    algebra run the world: <strong>span</strong> (everything reachable from your vectors),
    <strong>basis</strong> (a minimal complete set — two independent vectors span the whole
    plane, which is why E7.2's two coordinates describe any point), <strong>dimension</strong>
    (how long a basis is), and the rank–nullity theorem: what a map preserves and what it
    destroys always add up to the dimension you started with. Collinearity becomes
    linear dependence — three collinear points give vectors where one is a scalar multiple
    of another — and "prove ABCD is a trapezium" becomes "show one side vector is a multiple
    of the other".
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="find the smuggled assumption" widget="spot-error" config={laDiagSpot} />
  <p>
    Solving systems is the applied payoff of the same structure. A pair of simultaneous
    linear equations is the question "which point lies on both lines?" — two
    intersection conditions, solved by elimination, and Gaussian elimination is the
    systematic version: subtract multiples of one row from another (a shear! each row
    operation is an invertible transformation) until the system triangularises. The
    vocabulary transfers wholesale: a unique solution exists exactly when the
    coefficient matrix is invertible (non-zero determinant); no solution when the
    lines are parallel (inconsistent); infinitely many when the equations repeat
    (dependent). Linear algebra's economic value is that this analysis scales without
    change to millions of equations — structural engineering solvers, weather models
    and machine-learning training all spend their time in exactly this loop, at
    sizes where no picture is possible.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="pick the solver for 10,000 equations" widget="branch" config={laSolveBranch} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "matrices are a notational convenience for coordinates."
    They are the objects: a matrix <em>is</em> the transformation, valid in any basis, and
    the same algebra governs systems with millions of dimensions (an image, a population
    state, a web page's rank) where no picture exists. The 2×2 case is the sandbox, not the
    subject.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the magnitude of a sum is the sum of the magnitudes."
    |a + b| ≤ |a| + |b| is a genuine inequality — the triangle inequality — and it is strict
    almost always. Walk 3 km east then 4 km north and you are 5 km from home, not 7. Forgetting
    the difference between "path length" and "net displacement" is the classic physics and
    navigation error, and the inequality is the mathematics that catches it.
  </p>
  <p><strong>So far:</strong> vector geometry replaced coordinates with relationships, Gaussian elimination showed each row operation to be an invertible shear, and the solution-count cases mapped exactly onto the determinant story — with two misconceptions dispatched: matrices are objects, not notation, and magnitudes do not add. The remaining material — eigenvalues, the SVD, infinite dimensions — builds directly on this structure.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>State what a matrix's columns tell you about its transformation, and what determinants of 1, 0 and −1 each mean geometrically.</li>
    <li>Two ropes at 45° on either side of the vertical hold a 100 N weight. Compute the tension in each rope and explain why it exceeds 100 N.</li>
    <li>Why does matrix multiplication fail to commute? Give the lesson's transformation example and say what it implies about the order of operations.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) The columns are where (1, 0) and (0, 1) land — and since
    every vector is a combination of those two, the columns fix where <em>everything</em> goes.
    Determinant 1: area preserved (a rotation). Determinant 0: the plane collapses onto a line
    — a singular matrix, information destroyed, no inverse. Determinant −1: area preserved but
    orientation flipped (a reflection). (2) Equilibrium requires the upward components to
    cancel the weight: 2T cos 45° = 100, so T = 100 ÷ (2 × 0.7071) ≈ 70.7 N. It exceeds 100 N
    because each rope's vertical contribution is only cos 45° ≈ 0.71 of its tension — the
    shallower the angle, the smaller the useful fraction, hence about 288 N per rope at 80°
    from vertical. (3) Rotate 90° and then
    reflect in the x-axis lands in a different place from reflect-then-rotate; composition of
    transformations is matrix multiplication, and since the products differ, matrix
    multiplication is not commutative — order of operations is part of the data, not a
    convention.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A matrix <em>is</em> the transformation: its columns are the images of the basis vectors, which fixes everything else.</li>
    <li>The determinant is the signed area scale — 1 rotation, −1 reflection, 0 collapse — and invertibility lives exactly at non-zero.</li>
    <li>Composition of transformations is matrix multiplication, and the order matters: the algebra is not commutative.</li>
    <li>The dot product computes lengths, angles and work by arithmetic; projection is its geometric act, and least squares is a projection theorem.</li>
    <li>Vector geometry replaces coordinates with relationships: span, basis and dimension — with rank–nullity keeping the books.</li>
    <li>Gaussian elimination is a chain of shears — every row operation is invertible, and solution counts mirror the determinant cases.</li>
    <li>|a + b| ≤ |a| + |b| separates net displacement from path length — the triangle inequality catches the classic navigation error.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Two sibling expert lessons pick up the thread. <strong>Abstract algebra</strong> studies
    the transformations themselves as a group — why the invertible ones close under
    composition — and <strong>Complex analysis</strong> shows that a rotation in the plane is
    also a multiplication, merging this lesson's matrices with the exponential. Between the
    three, school transformations become the working language of games,
    statistics and quantum mechanics.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Eigenvalues: the directions that matter.</strong> For a matrix A, an eigenvector
    satisfies Av = λv — a direction the map only stretches, never rotates. They are the hidden
    skeleton of a transformation: repeated application of A (population models, interest
    rates, PageRank's random surfer) is dominated by the largest eigenvalue, and everything
    else decays relative to it. Google's original PageRank is the statement "the ranking
    vector is the eigenvector of the link matrix with eigenvalue 1" — a multi-billion-dollar
    corollary of E7.4's span-and-basis language. Stability analysis of any differential
    equation, from predator–prey models to bridge oscillation, is eigenvalue reading: negative
    real parts mean disturbances die out, positive mean they grow.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="true or false — reading eigenvalues" widget="judge" config={laEigenJudge} />
  <p>
    <strong>The SVD: every matrix decomposes.</strong> The singular value decomposition says
    any matrix whatsoever — square or not, invertible or not — factors into rotate, then
    scale along the axes, then rotate again. The scaling factors (singular values) rank the
    matrix's true information content, and keeping only the largest few is the mathematics of
    compression: it underlies PCA in statistics, image compression, noise reduction and the
    low-rank approximations inside recommendation systems. The Eckart–Young theorem makes it
    precise: truncating small singular values is provably the best possible approximation of
    that rank. When a data scientist "reduces dimensions", this is the theorem being invoked.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="eigen-decomposition versus SVD — drag the divider" widget="compare" config={laSvdCompare} />
  <p>
    <strong>Beyond the plane.</strong> The axioms of a vector space never mention numbers,
    arrows or 2D: functions can be vectors (Fourier series writes a signal as a combination
    of basis sines), polynomials can be, and quantum states must be — quantum mechanics lives
    in a Hilbert space, a vector space with the dot product generalised to an inner product,
    and "measurement" is projection onto a basis direction. Infinite-dimensional spaces bring
    genuine subtleties (basis, completeness and convergence begin to matter), but the
    geometric instincts built here — span, projection, orthogonality, determinant — survive
    intact. The 2×2 matrix in the widget above is, quite literally, the whole subject in
    miniature: four numbers, one map, one number (the determinant) that tells you whether
    anything was lost.
  </p>
  <Widget no="11" caption="pair each object with why it is a vector space" widget="match" config={laSpanMatch} />
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Axler, Linear Algebra Done Right; Strang, Linear Algebra and Its Applications. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
